SWTOR on Mac's changes on top of SWGL on Mac's two mouse patches
(patches/winemac-swgl-relative-mouse.patch, patches/winemac-swgl-input-thread.patch):

- RawMouseWhenCursorHidden and IgnoreStaleCursorWarps are off unless the registry turns them
  on; SWTOR on Mac turns RawMouseWhenCursorHidden on for swtor.exe only ("Raw mouse-look" in
  Settings) and leaves IgnoreStaleCursorWarps off: SWTOR calls SetCursorPos only to centre
  the pointer for mouse-look and to put it back where the click was, never to re-assert an
  old position as SWG does, and does not read DirectInput. The fallback that looks for one of
  our windows under the pointer is part of the option too, so with it off the driver behaves
  as the stock one.
- RawMouseMatchPointerSpeed (on by default): GCMouse's device counts are sent at the measured
  ratio of AppKit's motion (the macOS pointer: tracking speed, Retina scale) to GCMouse's, so
  mouse-look turns as fast as with AppKit's moves (SWTOR on Mac 0.0.x); until the first
  measurement AppKit's moves are sent. RawMouseGameControllerScale (Mouse-Look Speed) is a
  factor on that, for both sources.
- The PE side starts the input thread only if the unix side says it has it (init params'
  app_icon_callback set to MACDRV_HAS_RAW_INPUT_THREAD): installers that swap only winemac.so
  (SWTOR on Mac 0.1.0-dev.1 and earlier) can leave this PE side with an older unix side.
- Debug output: warn+rawmouse, a RAWMOUSE line a second while moves are sent (both sources,
  the measured ratio); warn+threadcpu, a THREADCPU line a second from the input thread: the
  CPU time of the process, its busiest threads and the session's wineserver (its task times
  are Mach ticks, 41.67 ns each on Apple silicon, converted with hw.tbfrequency), in the process
  whose ThreadCpuStats option is on (swtor.exe; the session's other processes inherit its
  WINEDEBUG and stderr).

